{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh1BiJxK4gJ9GLuqWWsN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For [Co(H₂O)₆]³⁺, **Dq ≈ 1898 cm⁻¹ and B ≈ 557 cm⁻¹**, which gives C ≈ 2505 cm⁻¹. These come from the strong-field formulas below, which ignore mixing with nearby states.\n\n**Approach**\n\n[Co(H₂O)₆]³⁺ is low-spin d⁶, so its ground state is ¹A₁g (t₂g⁶). Both spin-allowed bands come from moving one electron up, t₂g⁶ → t₂g⁵e_g¹. In the strong-field (first-order) picture, the transition energies are:\n\n- ¹A₁g → ¹T₁g: E₁ = 10Dq − C\n- ¹A₁g → ¹T₂g: E₂ = 10Dq + 16B − C\n\n**Solving**\n\n1. Subtract the two bands to get B:\n   E₂ − E₁ = 16B = 25 381 − 16 474 = 8 907 cm⁻¹\n   B = 8 907 / 16 ≈ **556.7 cm⁻¹**\n2. Use C/B = 4.5:\n   C = 4.5 × 556.7 ≈ **2 505 cm⁻¹**\n3. Use the first band to get Dq:\n   10Dq = E₁ + C = 16 474 + 2 505 ≈ 18 979 cm⁻¹\n   Dq ≈ **1 898 cm⁻¹**\n\n**Checks**\n\n- B is about 51% of the free-ion value for Co³⁺ (about 1100 cm⁻¹). This is a typical nephelauxetic reduction.\n- Dq/B ≈ 3.4, which is well above the high-spin/low-spin crossover for d⁶ (about 2.0). That is consistent with the low-spin assignment.\n\n**Limitation**\n\nThese formulas leave out mixing between states of the same symmetry (configuration interaction). A full diagonalisation of the Tanabe–Sugano matrices would shift the values by a small amount, roughly 1–3%.\n\n{\"Dq\": 1897.9, \"B\": 556.7, \"C\": 2505.1, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Low-spin d6 strong-field expressions: E1=10Dq-C, E2=10Dq+16B-C with C=4.5B.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":820,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":820,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":nu